 Normalization in Oracle (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, 4NF, 5NF) using Functional Dependencies

AIM
To study and implement the concept of Normalization (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, 4NF, 5NF) using Functional Dependencies, and to design and create normalized tables in Oracle using SQL.

1. THEORY
1.1 What is Normalization?
Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce data redundancy and avoid undesirable characteristics like Insertion, Update, and Deletion anomalies. It involves decomposing a large table into smaller, well-structured tables based on Functional Dependencies (FDs), and defining relationships between them.
1.2 Functional Dependency (FD)
A functional dependency X → Y (read as "X determines Y") means: for every value of attribute X, there is exactly one corresponding value of attribute Y. X is called the determinant, Y is the dependent.
Types of FD-related concepts used in normalization:
Term	Meaning
Prime Attribute	An attribute that is part of any candidate key
Non-Prime Attribute	An attribute that is not part of any candidate key
Full Functional Dependency	Y depends on the whole of composite key X, not a subset
Partial Dependency	Y depends on only part of a composite key
Transitive Dependency	X → Y and Y → Z implies X → Z (Z depends on X indirectly through Y)
Multivalued Dependency (MVD)	X →→ Y : for a given X, there is a set of values of Y, independent of other attributes
Join Dependency (JD)	A table can be reconstructed by joining multiple decomposed tables without loss of information
1.3 Normal Forms Summary
Normal Form	Rule	Removes
1NF	Atomic values only; no repeating groups/multivalued columns	Multivalued attributes
2NF	1NF + no partial dependency (applies when PK is composite)	Partial dependency
3NF	2NF + no transitive dependency	Transitive dependency
BCNF	3NF + every determinant is a candidate key	Anomalies from overlapping candidate keys
4NF	BCNF + no multivalued dependency	Multivalued dependency
5NF (PJNF)	4NF + no join dependency (lossless decomposition only)	Join dependency

2. UNNORMALIZED TABLE (UNF) — The Starting Point
Consider a college database storing student course enrollment details in a single flat table.
StudentCourse (Unnormalized)
SID	SName	Courses	Faculty	FacultyPhone	Dept	HOD
S1	Arun	DBMS, OS	Ravi, Meena	9990001, 9990002	CSE	Dr. Sharma
S2	Bala	DBMS	Ravi	9990001	CSE	Dr. Sharma
Problem: The Courses, Faculty, and FacultyPhone columns hold multiple (multivalued) values in a single cell → violates 1NF.
Functional Dependencies identified:
SID        → SName
SID, Course → Faculty
Faculty    → FacultyPhone, Dept
Dept       → HOD

3. FIRST NORMAL FORM (1NF)
Rule
Every attribute must contain a single atomic value; no repeating groups or multivalued/composite columns.
Step: Remove multivalued columns → create one row per (SID, Course)
StudentCourse_1NF
SID	SName	Course	Faculty	FacultyPhone	Dept	HOD
S1	Arun	DBMS	Ravi	9990001	CSE	Dr. Sharma
S1	Arun	OS	Meena	9990002	CSE	Dr. Sharma
S2	Bala	DBMS	Ravi	9990001	CSE	Dr. Sharma
Oracle SQL Implementation
sql
-- Table is now in 1NF: every cell holds a single atomic value
CREATE TABLE StudentCourse_1NF (
    SID           VARCHAR2(10),
    SName         VARCHAR2(30),
    Course        VARCHAR2(20),
    Faculty       VARCHAR2(30),
    FacultyPhone  VARCHAR2(15),
    Dept          VARCHAR2(10),
    HOD           VARCHAR2(30),
    CONSTRAINT pk_1nf PRIMARY KEY (SID, Course)
);

INSERT INTO StudentCourse_1NF VALUES ('S1','Arun','DBMS','Ravi','9990001','CSE','Dr. Sharma');
INSERT INTO StudentCourse_1NF VALUES ('S1','Arun','OS','Meena','9990002','CSE','Dr. Sharma');
INSERT INTO StudentCourse_1NF VALUES ('S2','Bala','DBMS','Ravi','9990001','CSE','Dr. Sharma');

COMMIT;

SELECT * FROM StudentCourse_1NF;
Still has problems: Composite key is (SID, Course). SName depends only on SID (part of the key) → partial dependency → violates 2NF.

4. SECOND NORMAL FORM (2NF)
Rule
Must be in 1NF and every non-prime attribute must be fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key (no partial dependency). Applies only when the PK is composite.
Functional Dependencies re-examined
SID              → SName                     (partial - depends only on SID)
SID, Course      → Faculty, FacultyPhone,
                    Dept, HOD                 (full - needs both SID & Course... 
                                                actually Faculty depends on Course too)
Course           → Faculty                    (partial - depends only on Course)
Faculty          → FacultyPhone, Dept
Dept             → HOD
Decomposition — split into 3 tables
Student
SID (PK)	SName
S1	Arun
S2	Bala
Course_Faculty
Course (PK)	Faculty	FacultyPhone	Dept	HOD
DBMS	Ravi	9990001	CSE	Dr. Sharma
OS	Meena	9990002	CSE	Dr. Sharma
Enrollment (bridge table)
SID (FK)	Course (FK)
S1	DBMS
S1	OS
S2	DBMS
Oracle SQL Implementation
sql
CREATE TABLE Student (
    SID    VARCHAR2(10) PRIMARY KEY,
    SName  VARCHAR2(30)
);

CREATE TABLE Course_Faculty (
    Course        VARCHAR2(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    Faculty       VARCHAR2(30),
    FacultyPhone  VARCHAR2(15),
    Dept          VARCHAR2(10),
    HOD           VARCHAR2(30)
);

CREATE TABLE Enrollment (
    SID     VARCHAR2(10),
    Course  VARCHAR2(20),
    PRIMARY KEY (SID, Course),
    FOREIGN KEY (SID) REFERENCES Student(SID),
    FOREIGN KEY (Course) REFERENCES Course_Faculty(Course)
);

INSERT INTO Student VALUES ('S1','Arun');
INSERT INTO Student VALUES ('S2','Bala');

INSERT INTO Course_Faculty VALUES ('DBMS','Ravi','9990001','CSE','Dr. Sharma');
INSERT INTO Course_Faculty VALUES ('OS','Meena','9990002','CSE','Dr. Sharma');

INSERT INTO Enrollment VALUES ('S1','DBMS');
INSERT INTO Enrollment VALUES ('S1','OS');
INSERT INTO Enrollment VALUES ('S2','DBMS');

COMMIT;
Still has problems: In Course_Faculty: Course → Faculty → FacultyPhone, Dept and Dept → HOD. So Course → Dept → HOD is a transitive dependency → violates 3NF.

5. THIRD NORMAL FORM (3NF)
Rule
Must be in 2NF and no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on the primary key (no non-key attribute determines another non-key attribute).
Decompose Course_Faculty further
Course_Faculty_3NF
Course (PK)	Faculty (FK)
DBMS	Ravi
OS	Meena
Faculty_Dept
Faculty (PK)	FacultyPhone	Dept (FK)
Ravi	9990001	CSE
Meena	9990002	CSE
Department
Dept (PK)	HOD
CSE	Dr. Sharma
Oracle SQL Implementation
sql
CREATE TABLE Department (
    Dept  VARCHAR2(10) PRIMARY KEY,
    HOD   VARCHAR2(30)
);

CREATE TABLE Faculty_Dept (
    Faculty       VARCHAR2(30) PRIMARY KEY,
    FacultyPhone  VARCHAR2(15),
    Dept          VARCHAR2(10),
    FOREIGN KEY (Dept) REFERENCES Department(Dept)
);

CREATE TABLE Course_3NF (
    Course   VARCHAR2(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    Faculty  VARCHAR2(30),
    FOREIGN KEY (Faculty) REFERENCES Faculty_Dept(Faculty)
);

INSERT INTO Department VALUES ('CSE','Dr. Sharma');

INSERT INTO Faculty_Dept VALUES ('Ravi','9990001','CSE');
INSERT INTO Faculty_Dept VALUES ('Meena','9990002','CSE');

INSERT INTO Course_3NF VALUES ('DBMS','Ravi');
INSERT INTO Course_3NF VALUES ('OS','Meena');

COMMIT;

-- Verify with a join
SELECT c.Course, f.Faculty, f.FacultyPhone, f.Dept, d.HOD
FROM Course_3NF c
JOIN Faculty_Dept f ON c.Faculty = f.Faculty
JOIN Department d ON f.Dept = d.Dept;
At this point the schema is in 3NF (and also satisfies BCNF here, since every determinant — Course, Faculty, Dept — is a candidate/primary key of its own table).

6. FOURTH NORMAL FORM (4NF)
Rule
Must be in BCNF and have no multivalued dependency (MVD) — i.e., no row has two or more independent multivalued facts about the same entity stored together.
Example scenario
Suppose a Faculty member can teach multiple Courses and also knows multiple Languages, and these two facts are independent of each other.
Faculty_Skills (violates 4NF)
Faculty	Course	Language
Ravi	DBMS	English
Ravi	DBMS	Hindi
Ravi	OS	English
Ravi	OS	Hindi
Here Faculty →→ Course and Faculty →→ Language are two independent MVDs mixed in one table, causing redundancy (every Course is paired with every Language).
Decomposition into two independent tables
Faculty_Course
Faculty	Course
Ravi	DBMS
Ravi	OS
Faculty_Language
Faculty	Language
Ravi	English
Ravi	Hindi
Oracle SQL Implementation
sql
CREATE TABLE Faculty_Course (
    Faculty  VARCHAR2(30),
    Course   VARCHAR2(20),
    PRIMARY KEY (Faculty, Course)
);

CREATE TABLE Faculty_Language (
    Faculty   VARCHAR2(30),
    Language  VARCHAR2(20),
    PRIMARY KEY (Faculty, Language)
);

INSERT INTO Faculty_Course VALUES ('Ravi','DBMS');
INSERT INTO Faculty_Course VALUES ('Ravi','OS');

INSERT INTO Faculty_Language VALUES ('Ravi','English');
INSERT INTO Faculty_Language VALUES ('Ravi','Hindi');

COMMIT;
Now each table stores exactly one independent multivalued fact → satisfies 4NF.

7. FIFTH NORMAL FORM (5NF) / PROJECT-JOIN NORMAL FORM (PJNF)
Rule
Must be in 4NF and contain no join dependency that is not implied by the candidate keys — meaning the table cannot be further decomposed into smaller tables without losing information (it must be reconstructable by joining the decomposed parts with no spurious rows).
Example scenario
Suppose a table records which Faculty teaches which Course, using which Textbook — but not every combination is valid; the relationship is a genuine three-way constraint (a Faculty teaches a Course only using specific Textbooks, and this cannot be derived from just two pairwise tables).
Faculty_Course_Book (violates 5NF)
Faculty	Course	Textbook
Ravi	DBMS	Korth
Ravi	DBMS	Navathe
Meena	OS	Silberschatz
If we decompose this into only two pairwise tables (Faculty-Course and Course-Textbook), rejoining them may generate spurious/incorrect combinations that never existed in the original data. This is only correctly decomposable using all three projections.
Decomposition into three tables (lossless join)
Faculty_Course
Faculty	Course
Ravi	DBMS
Meena	OS
Course_Textbook
Course	Textbook
DBMS	Korth
DBMS	Navathe
OS	Silberschatz
Faculty_Textbook
Faculty	Textbook
Ravi	Korth
Ravi	Navathe
Meena	Silberschatz
Oracle SQL Implementation
sql
CREATE TABLE Faculty_Course_5NF (
    Faculty  VARCHAR2(30),
    Course   VARCHAR2(20),
    PRIMARY KEY (Faculty, Course)
);

CREATE TABLE Course_Textbook_5NF (
    Course     VARCHAR2(20),
    Textbook   VARCHAR2(30),
    PRIMARY KEY (Course, Textbook)
);

CREATE TABLE Faculty_Textbook_5NF (
    Faculty    VARCHAR2(30),
    Textbook   VARCHAR2(30),
    PRIMARY KEY (Faculty, Textbook)
);

INSERT INTO Faculty_Course_5NF VALUES ('Ravi','DBMS');
INSERT INTO Faculty_Course_5NF VALUES ('Meena','OS');

INSERT INTO Course_Textbook_5NF VALUES ('DBMS','Korth');
INSERT INTO Course_Textbook_5NF VALUES ('DBMS','Navathe');
INSERT INTO Course_Textbook_5NF VALUES ('OS','Silberschatz');

INSERT INTO Faculty_Textbook_5NF VALUES ('Ravi','Korth');
INSERT INTO Faculty_Textbook_5NF VALUES ('Ravi','Navathe');
INSERT INTO Faculty_Textbook_5NF VALUES ('Meena','Silberschatz');

COMMIT;

-- Reconstruct the original relation via a 3-way natural join (lossless join test)
SELECT fc.Faculty, fc.Course, ct.Textbook
FROM Faculty_Course_5NF fc
JOIN Course_Textbook_5NF ct ON fc.Course = ct.Course
JOIN Faculty_Textbook_5NF ft ON fc.Faculty = ft.Faculty AND ct.Textbook = ft.Textbook;
The table is now in 5NF — it cannot be decomposed further without loss of information, and rejoining the three tables reproduces exactly the original valid combinations.

8. VERIFYING NORMALIZATION IN ORACLE (Useful Commands)
sql
-- View all tables created in this schema
SELECT table_name FROM user_tables;

-- View column structure of a table
DESC Faculty_Dept;

-- View primary key / foreign key constraints
SELECT constraint_name, constraint_type, table_name
FROM user_constraints
WHERE table_name IN ('STUDENT','COURSE_3NF','FACULTY_DEPT','DEPARTMENT');

-- View functional-dependency-driven referential integrity
SELECT a.table_name, a.column_name, c.constraint_type
FROM user_cons_columns a
JOIN user_constraints c ON a.constraint_name = c.constraint_name
WHERE c.constraint_type IN ('P','R')
ORDER BY a.table_name;

9. RESULT
Thus, the given unnormalized relation was successfully decomposed into 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, 4NF, and 5NF using functional dependency analysis, and the corresponding normalized tables were created, populated, and verified in Oracle.
